<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Qutayba ibn Muslim</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">669–<a href="/years/715">715</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Qutayba ibn Muslim was an Arab general and Umayyad governor of Khurasan from 705, appointed by al-Hajjaj. In a decade of campaigns he subdued <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokharistan">Tokharistan</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> in 709 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khwarazm">Khwarazm</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a> in 712, and raided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Ferghana</a>, bringing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/transoxiana">Transoxiana</a> under Muslim rule. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caliphate">caliph</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-walid-i">al-Walid I</a> died in 715, Qutayba rebelled against his successor Sulayman and was killed by his own troops.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the east, <b>Qutayba ibn Muslim</b> conquered Bukhara and Samarkand in Transoxiana, and Muhammad ibn Qasim led the Umayyad conquest of Sind in 711–712.</blockquote><a href="/empire/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In the west, Arab armies of the Umayyad Caliphate under <b>Qutayba ibn Muslim</b> took Bukhara in 709 and Samarkand in 712.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Arab general <b>Qutayba ibn Muslim</b> conquered Khwarazm in 712, the shahs remained as vassals, converted to Islam and ruled under Caliphal and later Samanid authority, while a rival centre grew at Gurganj.</blockquote><a href="/state/afrighid-khwarazm">Afrighid Khwarazm</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Qutayba ibn Muslim</b> captured Bukhara in 709 and installed the young Tughshada as a vassal Bukhar Khudah, building the first mosque.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-bukhara">Principality of Bukhara</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese records list embassies to the Tang court, and Arab armies under <b>Qutayba ibn Muslim</b> campaigned there from 713.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-ferghana">Principality of Ferghana</a></li><li><blockquote>Arab armies conquered Kesh during the campaigns of <b>Qutayba ibn Muslim</b> in the early 8th century, and it was absorbed into Umayyad and Abbasid Transoxiana.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-kesh">Principality of Kesh</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/iraq">Iraq</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fergana-valley">Ferghana Valley</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=30.52,47.81&amp;year=698" aria-label="Basra on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5789 1050 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Qutayba ibn Muslim"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6239 1320h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6239 1320h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=30.52,47.81&amp;year=698">What was here in 698 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
